<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James, brother of Jesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 62 or 69 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as James the Just, James the Righteous, James Adelphotheos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James, called the Just, was according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> and the first leader of the church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. Paul knew him personally and mentions him in his letter to the Galatians. Accounts of his death differ: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josephus">Josephus</a> records that he was stoned in 62 CE on the order of the high priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ananus-ben-ananus">Ananus ben Ananus</a>, while Hegesippus places his death in 69 and says he was thrown from the Temple pinnacle and clubbed. Catholic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> teaching holds that the brothers of Jesus were cousins or stepbrothers, while others consider James a son of Mary and Joseph. Most agree he is not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-the-great">James, son of Zebedee</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-brother-of-jesus.jpg" alt="James, brother of Jesus" width="250" height="353" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_James_the_Just.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
